    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, known chiefly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, with its scale debated by historians and archaeologists.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> the capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> built its temple, making the city a political and religious centre.</li><li>Its memory as a golden age shaped Jewish, Christian and Islamic ideas of kingship and messianic hope.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The earliest reference to the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>” outside the Bible appears on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscription found in northern Israel in 1993.</p></aside>
    </section>

