    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saul.jpg" alt="Saul" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saul_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernst Josephson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Almost everything known about the United Monarchy comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, chiefly the books of Samuel and Kings, written down in their present form centuries later, so its dates and scale are uncertain. According to this account, the Israelite tribes of the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> lived without kings in the period of the Judges, but growing pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> of the coastal plain, who captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, led them to demand a king. The prophet Samuel anointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> of the tribe of Benjamin, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibeah">Gibeah</a>; the traditional chronology places his accession in the late eleventh century BCE, around 1047 or 1050 BCE. Saul won early victories, relieving the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabesh-gilead">Jabesh-Gilead</a> from the Ammonites and fighting the Philistines, but he fell with his sons at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mount-gilboa">Battle of Mount Gilboa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David of Bethlehem</a>, a former commander of Saul who had quarrelled with him, was then made king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, while Saul’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ish-bosheth">Ish-bosheth</a> ruled the northern tribes. After Ish-bosheth’s murder the elders of Israel also accepted David, and around 1000 BCE he captured the Jebusite city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, Urusalim of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, and made it the capital of a united kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerusalem.jpg" alt="Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Mount_(Aerial_view,_2007)_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The society of the United Monarchy was overwhelmingly rural. Archaeology shows that the central highlands were dotted with small villages of farmers and herders, who grew wheat, barley, olives and vines on terraced hillsides, stored water in plastered cisterns and kept sheep and goats. Kinship was the basis of social life: households belonged to clans and tribes, and land was held by families. The biblical account describes growing royal wealth under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, financed by taxation, forced labour and trade. According to 1 Kings, he exchanged wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> for cedar and cypress from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and Phoenician craftsmen, sent ships with Phoenician sailors from Ezion-geber on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophir">Ophir</a> for gold, and traded in horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>; the visit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a> belongs to the same tradition. Historians debate how much of this reflects tenth-century reality, since the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was small and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> modest in the period. Religion centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> as the national god, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a> brought to Jerusalem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and housed in the temple built by Solomon, though worship at local shrines and of other deities continued, as later prophets complained.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mount-gilboa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mount-gilboa.jpg" alt="Battle of Mount Gilboa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mount-gilboa">Battle of Mount Gilboa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saul_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernst Josephson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The monarchy was born of war. According to the Bible, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> led a tribal militia against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, Ammonites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalekites">Amalekites</a>, but the Philistines, with their iron weapons and fortified cities, remained superior, and Saul was defeated and killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mount-gilboa">Battle of Mount Gilboa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> built a more professional force. His personal companions, the “mighty men”, and a royal guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherethites-and-pelethites">Cherethites and Pelethites</a>, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> or Philistine origin, formed its core, while his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joab">Joab</a> commanded the levy of the tribes. The biblical narrative credits David with breaking Philistine power and subjugating Moab, Ammon and Edom, and with defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadadezer-of-zobah">Hadadezer of Aram-Zobah</a> and his Aramean allies, creating a zone of tributary states from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He also had to suppress revolts at home, notably that of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalom">Absalom</a> and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheba-son-of-bichri">Sheba son of Bichri</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> is said to have kept a force of 1,400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and 12,000 horsemen and to have fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, Hazor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> and Gezer. Archaeologists once attributed the six-chambered gates of these cities to Solomon, but their date is now disputed, with many scholars placing them in the ninth century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/david"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david.jpg" alt="David" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David,_the_King_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerard van Honthorst, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Monarchy marked the transition from a loose tribal society to a royal state, a change the Bible itself presents with ambivalence, as Samuel warns the people that a king will take their sons, daughters, fields and harvests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> seems to have ruled as a war leader with little administration beyond his household. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, according to the books of Samuel, created a court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with a commander of the army, a recorder, scribes, priests and a chief of forced labour, and took a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of the people, which the narrative condemns. Kingship was dynastic but contested: the succession to David was fought out among his sons, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> secured the throne around 970 BCE through the support of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathan-prophet">Nathan</a>, the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadok">Zadok</a> and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathsheba">Bathsheba</a> against his older brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonijah">Adonijah</a>. The Bible describes Solomon dividing Israel into twelve administrative districts, each supplying the court for one month a year, and imposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour for building projects, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple of Jerusalem</a> and his palace. He also cemented alliances through marriages, including to a daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. The historicity of these institutions is debated, but some form of royal administration based in Jerusalem probably existed by the tenth century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele.jpg" alt="Tel Dan stele" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRSLM_300116_Tel_Dan_Stele_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oren Rozen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Whatever its actual extent, the United Monarchy had an immense afterlife. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a>, a ninth-century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscription found in 1993, refers to the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>”, confirming that a dynasty claiming descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> within about a century and a half of his reign. The biblical portrait of David as warrior, poet and founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> as the wise builder of the Temple, became central to Jewish identity. The prophetic promise that David’s line would rule forever shaped the hope for a future anointed king, the messiah, which passed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is presented as the son of David, and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, where David (Dawud) and Solomon (Sulayman) are revered as prophets and kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalms</a> were traditionally attributed in large part to David, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-proverbs">Proverbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastes">Ecclesiastes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a> to Solomon. Jerusalem’s status as a holy city for three religions begins with David’s capture of the city and Solomon’s temple. In modern times the United Monarchy has been the subject of intense debate between scholars who regard it as a substantial state and those, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a>, who see it as a small highland chiefdom later idealised by Judahite writers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon.jpg" alt="Solomon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simeon_Solomon,_King_Solomon,_1872_or_1874,_NGA_76152_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simeon Solomon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to the Bible, the unity of the kingdom was already strained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>’s later years. His taxation and forced labour bore heavily on the northern tribes, and the Bible also blames his foreign wives and their gods. External enemies reappeared: Hadad, an Edomite prince who had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, returned to stir up Edom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezon">Rezon</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and founded an Aramean kingdom hostile to Israel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam</a>, an official in charge of forced labour for the house of Joseph, rebelled and fled to Egypt, where he was protected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shishak</a>, usually identified with Shoshenq I. When Solomon died around 930 BCE, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rehoboam">Rehoboam</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> to be acclaimed by the northern tribes. They demanded lighter burdens; Rehoboam refused, reportedly declaring that he would chastise them with scorpions where his father had used whips, and the northern tribes stoned his chief of forced labour and made Jeroboam their king. The realm split into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> in the north and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the south, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> remaining the capital of Judah under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>. A few years later Shoshenq I campaigned in the region, as recorded in his inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, known almost entirely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and debated by archaeologists, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> a royal and religious capital, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>, and became the model of kingship and messianic hope for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</em></p>

